| 140 | the library's `strToFloat` mis-parses a sign and overflows on a long mantissa: `strToFloat "-1.5"` is `-28.5`, and a 17-digit mantissa dies with `Overflow of ZZ32 100000000000000000` | NEGATIVE-VERIFIED | library bug (library) | silent | none | run-c | `strToFloat` (`Library/FortressLibrary.fss:4606-4611` at c124c6b65, "Quick and dirty implementation") sums `10^k (both[\|both\| - 1 - k].codePoint - '0'.codePoint)` under `SUM[\ZZ32\]` over every character, sign included, so `-` counts as -3 and `"-1.5"` is (-3*100 + 1*10 + 5)/10 = -28.5; and `10^k` is a `ZZ32`, which overflows at the 10th digit; `"1.5"` parses right. Workaround: `parseFloat` in `explorations/run-c/src/FlatData.fss:13` (digits into an `RR64` mantissa, a power of ten, `-` and the high minus, an `e`/`E` exponent). Probe `explorations/run-c/probes/g140_strtofloat.fss`, in the parent of 4ebf80aec; full text: history, row 140 |
| 338 | `walk` refuses `d: ZZ32 = -2147483648`, which is in range for the type, with `RHS expression type Long is not assignable to LHS type ZZ32`, where the compiled path accepts it and prints the value | NEGATIVE-VERIFIED | implementation gap (walk) | `Specification/basic/expressions/literals.tex`, "Literals" | none | repair batch R2's skeptic | Workaround, the only spelling of the minimum that works on both paths: `-2147483647 - 1`, as the library's `minimum` getters do (`Library/FortressLibrary.fss:736`, `:826` at c124c6b65). Not yet established: where walk decides the numeral's type (the message names `Long`: the negation is typed before the assignment), and whether `ZZ64 = -9223372036854775808` is refused too. Of the wider probe's five negative edges walk refuses only this one. Probes `explorations/compile-ladder/repair-r2-literal-wrap/probes/skeptic/s1.fss` and `explorations/compile-ladder/repair-r2-literal-wrap/probes/skeptic/s7.fss`, with their walk and compiled output, in the parent of ab067d9b6. full text: history, row 338 |
| 579 | against the one library the compiled checker refuses the floor and ceiling brackets of a `ZZ32` and of a numeral as an ambiguous coercion, and types `round` of a `ZZ32` at `QQ`'s `ZZ` | NEGATIVE-VERIFIED | library gap vs spec (library) | `Specification/basic-lib/basic-integers.tex`, "Integers" | none | climb batch 8 rung Q's skeptic and repair round | `Integral`'s body declares both brackets and `round(self)` (`Library/FortressLibrary.fss:725-727` at c124c6b65) and its api (`Library/FortressLibrary.fsi:449`) does not, so only `RR64`'s and `QQ`'s apply, by coercion: "Ambiguous coercion ... (RR64 & {Float, FloatLiteral})->ZZ64; QQ->ZZ." and "Function body has type ZZ, but declared return type is ZZ32.". Repair: `Integral`'s api states the three, `IntLiteral` its own. No gated program sees it before the switch-over: `explorations/reviews/numerics-plan-coordinator/probes-B/check.sh FloorBracketCk base` and `edit3`, (parent of 84aef6a4f), 5 errors each (`compile-ladder/rung-numeral-library/REPORT.md` section 6). full text: history, row 579 |
| 321 | **an object declaring or inheriting a zero-argument Fortress member named `toString` cannot be rendered on the compiled path: it recurses until the stack overflows**; walk prints its type name | NEGATIVE-VERIFIED | implementation gap (runtime) | `Specification/basic-lib/objects.tex`, "Objects" | `ProjectFortress/compiler_tests/XXXFortToStringRungS.test` | repair batch R1; climb batch 3 rung S's second skeptic | Codegen emits the member as `toString()Lfortress/CompilerBuiltin$String;`. The default `asString` is `jDefaultAsString(self)` (`ProjectFortress/LibraryBuiltin/CompilerBuiltin.fss:355` at c124c6b65), bound to `defaultAsString`, whose `hasOwnToString` (`ProjectFortress/src/com/sun/fortress/nativeHelpers/stringOps.java:108-118`) answers true, so `a.toString()` (`:109`) reaches `FValue.toString`, which re-enters `asString`. An object with no such member prints its type name (`DefaultRenderRungS`). Workaround: give the member another name. Blocks adopting walk's `trait Object`, which declares `getter toString` (`ProjectFortress/LibraryBuiltin/FortressBuiltin.fss:51`). full text: history, row 321 |
| 369 | **walk renders a tuple with "," between its elements, where the specification requires ", "**: `println((1, "a"))` prints `(1,a)`, and a seven-element tuple has no rendering: it ends in `CastError` | NEGATIVE-VERIFIED | implementation gap (library) | `Specification/basic-lib/objects.tex`, "Objects" | `ProjectFortress/tests/XXXTupleSeparatorRungS.fss` | climb batch 3 rung S's second skeptic | siblings: 374. `trait String`'s five `opr \|\|` overloads on two- to six-element tuples each write `","` (`Library/FortressLibrary.fss:4425-4433` at c124c6b65); `println(a:Any)` is `println("" a)` (`:4549`). Not `FTupleLike.java:60`, a diagnostic's text. Seven elements fall to `opr \|\|(a:String, b:Any)`, `cast[\Object\](b).asString` (`Library/FortressLibrary.fss:4488`), and a tuple is no `Object` (`ProjectFortress/tests/XXXTupleSevenRungS.fss`). Compiled prints `( 1 ,  a )` by juxtaposition (`ProjectFortress/LibraryBuiltin/CompilerBuiltin.fss:471`, row 76); with row 76 repaired it matches. The one-element `tuple` prefix is not measured. full text: history, row 369 |
| 374 | **a function value cannot be printed on either path**: `println(inc)` for `inc(x: ZZ32): ZZ32`, and `println(fn (x: ZZ32): ZZ32 => x + 2)`, end in `CastError` under walk, and the compiled checker refuses both calls | NEGATIVE-VERIFIED | implementation gap (library) | `Specification/basic-lib/objects.tex`, "Objects"; `Specification/basic/types-vals-vars.tex`, "Relationships between Types" | `ProjectFortress/tests/XXXFnRenderRungS.fss` | climb batch 3 rung S's skeptic | siblings: 369. Walk: `println(a:Any)` is `println("" a)` (`Library/FortressLibrary.fss:4549` at c124c6b65), and `opr \|\|(a:String, b:Any)` (`:4488`) is `cast[\Object\](b).asString`, for the `asString` of `trait Object` (`ProjectFortress/LibraryBuiltin/FortressBuiltin.fss:37-41`), which a function value is not. Compiled: "`Object->()` is not applicable to an argument of type `ZZ32->ZZ32`", from `println(x:Object)` (`ProjectFortress/LibraryBuiltin/CompilerBuiltin.fss:460`); that half turns on a conflict: `types-vals-vars.tex` puts arrow types under `Any` beside `Object`, `basic-lib/objects.tex` makes `Object` the root of all user-constructed objects and functions. full text: history, row 374 |
| 75 | `nanoTime()` has a different type on the two paths, `RR64` compiled and `ZZ64` under walk, so a program that passes its result where a `ZZ64` is expected does not compile | NEGATIVE-VERIFIED | library gap vs spec (prelude) | `library/default-libraries.tex`, "FortressLibrary" (subsection Top-level primitives, typeset from `Library/FortressLibrary.fsi`) | `explorations/tparallel.fss` | ours | Walk runs it; `bin/fortress compile tparallel.fss` refuses its `ms(t0, t1)`, declared over `ZZ64`: `(ZZ64, ZZ64)->ZZ64 is not applicable to an argument of type (RR64, RR64)`. `ProjectFortress/LibraryBuiltin/CompilerBuiltin.fsi:23` declares `nanoTime(): RR64` (body `CompilerBuiltin.fss:352`); `Library/FortressLibrary.fsi:2563` at c124c6b65, from which the specification's section is typeset, declares `ZZ64`. The prelude is deleted at the switch-over, when the compiled path takes the interpreter's library. `Library/FortressLibrary.fss:4522`'s `nanoTime():ZZ64` names `simpleLongArith.longNanoTime`, a Java `long`, which the checker reads as `ZZ64`; the prelude's `RR64` one stays. |
| 76 | G6 — string juxtaposition inserts spaces on the compiled path, silently changing almost every program's output | NEGATIVE-VERIFIED | library bug (prelude) | `Specification/basic/operators/operators.tex`, "Operators"; `Specification/basic/expressions/constant.tex`, "Types of Static Expressions" | `ProjectFortress/compiler_tests/XXXJuxtConcatRungS.test` | ours | One token: `ProjectFortress/LibraryBuiltin/CompilerBuiltin.fss:406` defines `String` juxtaposition as `self \|\|\| b`, the concatenation that inserts a space, where `Library/FortressLibrary.fss:4512-4514` at c124c6b65 use `\|\|`. Walk `k=5`, compiled `k= 5`. The chapter makes a string's juxtaposition `\|\|`, with no separator. The ladder file `tests/simplify1.fss`, which the code generator compiles, prints FAIL by this row: its `joinString(a, b) = a b` answers "cat dog". Link test `ProjectFortress/compiler_tests/JuxtConcatRungSLink.test`; the expected failure goes red on a local fix of the token. Workaround: write `\|\|`, as rows 314 and 316 do. full text: history, row 76 |
| 458 | a program's own `p1 ANDCOND p2` of two relational predicates is not a relational predicate under walk: `relational[\ZZ32\](p1 ANDCOND p2)` is `false`, though the condition's value is right and `filter` still fuses the two | NEGATIVE-VERIFIED | design limit (library) | silent | none | climb batch 7 rung H | `filter` fuses via `andRelCond`, called by `andCondCombine` (`Library/FortressLibrary.fss:4909-4916` at c124c6b65) from `SimpleFilterGenerator2` (`:4860`, `:4862`); `relational` reads a declared arrow type (`Library/Generator2.fss:88-93`), and the program's `ANDCOND` answers a plain `Condition[\()\]`. An `ANDCOND` on relational predicates beside the api's would be legal under walk only by `RelationalPredicateCondition[\E\] excludes Condition[\()\]`, which the checker refuses. Workaround: call `andRelCond` directly. Probe `explorations/compile-ladder/rung-exclusion-remainder/probes/values/HValues.fss`, in the parent of ab067d9b6; full text: history, row 458 |
